  "doc": [
    "*Carabus violaceus*, dorsal — the violet ground beetle.",
    "",
    "The third plate traced from Emil Hochdanz's lithographs for Calwer's",
    "*Käferbuch* (1876), after the stag beetle and the seven-spot, and by now the",
    "reason for staying with one engraver is visible on the contact sheet: three",
    "beetles that look like three beetles rather than three projects.",
    "`references/carabus-violaceus.jpg` is the file and `references/SOURCES.md`",
    "has the provenance.",
    "",
    "## What the drawing has to get right",
    "",
    "The **border**. This beetle is not violet; it is black with a violet rim,",
    "the outer margin of each wing case turned up and catching the light, and",
    "that rim is the whole of the common name. It is drawn as a `strip` — the",
    "outer margin traced, and a depth inwards from it — rather than as a second",
    "outline, because a band that keeps its width round a curve is a border and",
    "one that does not is a wedge.",
    "",
    "Then the **legs**, which are the other half of the animal. A ground beetle is",
    "a runner: the femora are long, the tibiae longer, and the hind tarsi reach a",
    "third of a body length past the wing cases. Drawn at a chafer's proportions",
    "it becomes a dung beetle.",
    "",
    "Then the **antennae**, which are filiform and are drawn segment by segment.",
    "Eleven on the animal; seven here, because at eighty pixels eleven capsules",
    "end-to-end is a dotted line. What has to read is that they are a chain of",
    "equal joints rather than a club or a comb, and seven says that.",
    "",
    "## Plate space",
    "",
    "y = 0 at the front margin of the head, y = 1000 at the elytral apex, which",
    "puts the pronotum at 200–410 and the wing cases at 400–1000 — the",
    "proportions measured off the lithograph, where the body is 405 pixels long",
    "and 157 across. The mandibles and the antennae reach above y = 0 and the",
    "hind tarsi past y = 1290; the frame is measured from the drawing.",
    "",
    "Drawing order is back to front: legs, then the wing cases and their surface",
    "work, then the thorax, then the head and its appendages."
  ],
  "parts": [
    {
      "note": [
        "The legs first, so they pass under the body.",
        "",
        "Long and slender, and measured that way: a Carabus runs down its prey on",
        "the ground rather than digging or clinging, and the reference shows all",
        "six legs held clear of the body with nothing foreshortened. Femora at",
        "about nine per cent of the width across the wing cases and tibiae at six,",
        "which is finer than the chafer's and heavier than the ladybird's."
      ],
